}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}^{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)=2, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=t, &  & b(t)=-1, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}^{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42eb79ee-12fc-5ebe-ae84-5140c7e8f9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312508} {:answer/id #uuid "5eed0038-5857-5699-b170-89ae78c852e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312733} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ca7dfa-d660-570a-8248-4046d8e69f27" :answ