/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c07460d-1eec-5672-90f4-7116bb6897fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315377} {:answer/id #uuid "5e82cbb5-72e9-5b8b-95fb-01801ff4b6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315650} {:answer/id #uuid "877cb6e8-1876-5f80-a0b2-98a2462c4775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315878} {:answer/id #uuid "a54e12c5-211f-5ac8-b470-1cfd82428117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316049} {:answer/id #uuid "b1cd5717-22ff-5564-89cb-a84b93a092d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c07460d-1eec-5672-90f4-7116bb6897fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315377} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ac1933-5ac4-59df-9437-0db31d242812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "278579d2-6c97-5a07-b8af-1fbac0d66174" :question/math-academy-id "q-34174" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4bd3b326c89316bc916658421bcd4c65c32a72672af9fbd7aa5397cb53ab374.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b7d38b-59e8-5c99-baf1-8ebca2bb3e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315394} {:answer/id #uuid "33432922-1ca0-5ab4-81d5-8b2d3857e426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315417} {:answer/id #uuid "36e2aefa-28e8-537d-a0b0-a16e4b71dc0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315437} {:answer/id #uuid "697157b6-d0b7-514a-85b2-51f3d2ff6cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315714} {:answer/id #uuid "f5260582-e5a1-582d-8cf8-b708b93e1374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33432922-1ca0-5ab4-81d5-8b2d3857e426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315417} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe195ec3-885a-500a-a80c-d7dfafea1cf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8ba2fd2-65b4-550d-8354-517b0631809b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342275" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{(x+4)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-4$\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{x+4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-4,$ since both denominators at $x=-4$ equal zero. So, $x=-4$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d51af1-e503-5a7e-b5c4-3ae217bde11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315305} {:answer/id #uuid "24800d2a-5fc1-582a-9fd9-908f2235d74f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315323} {:answer/id #uuid "317ac0b1-2810-51b7-8d45-3ccec2ac879c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315401} {:answer/id #uuid "a902dbd9-63b6-5ef6-bbe8-ce3be56fbbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316067} {:answer/id #uuid "ac091dc7-3951-5fcc-8f2c-d6262ff6a701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "317ac0b1-2810-51b7-8d45-3ccec2ac879c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315401} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c9a3fe6-a8ea-5181-ac25-7f8ac8fec6c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "905b994c-71d1-59e7-beab-bb9f96b34458" :question/math-academy-id "q-342279" :question/problem "$$\n(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-3}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{2x}{x+1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-3}{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{2x}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-1,$ since both denominators at $x=-1$ equal zero. So, $x=-1$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=3.$ So, $x=3$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "325de50a-bd2e-5b08-8def-2a320b8858b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315411} {:answer/id #uuid "43571603-dcf9-5d5f-b4d5-068e7b710ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315507} {:answer/id #uuid "4960eaa9-2ef2-55f9-9e74-72b970724f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315540} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ca10b7-c88f-59b6-99d2-5015abb15acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316370} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc1a8c-df7b-5734-81b5-5015a8867753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc1a8c-df7b-5734-81b5-5015a8867753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316485} :answer-field/id #uuid "0998a080-e070-520b-b84d-c64f605ce95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aae5f1f1-0d61-5bac-bdd4-df8a71221a89" :question/math-academy-id "q-342290" :question/problem "$$\n(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-2)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-1}{(x-2)(x+3)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x-2)(x+3)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=2,$ since both denominators at $x=2$ equal zero. So, $x=2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3778da-c7c9-52dd-a4f4-b677913ed30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315211} {:answer/id #uuid "4af8856d-b077-55be-a4c5-06637c7bf5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315546} {:answer/id #uuid "5897673e-eff1-5308-8269-e1dce86b32e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315627} {:answer/id #uuid "6f027614-3414-5502-b639-6fd727041625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315744} {:answer/id #uuid "bac4018c-c178-5e74-9117-963f97849d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f3778da-c7c9-52dd-a4f4-b677913ed30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315211} :answer-field/id #uuid "be915304-5fb5-5bd3-87d9-6bbdc4bd3a54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84a30812-4c38-529a-be0d-6e8e7676cbe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-342295" :question/problem "$$\n(x+3)(x-2){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+3)(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x-2){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=1.$ So, $x=1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28cb6212-1c96-5038-9cf1-1238bb9a8ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315352} {:answer/id #uuid "57690396-7e42-5f88-8067-3e0a26680548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315625} {:answer/id #uuid "82fb4501-cf3a-522e-827f-c28d838cb604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315842} {:answer/id #uuid "a2429bd6-3fbb-5688-9cac-94527928908e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316032} {:answer/id #uuid "aa99557a-eba6-5522-a1e9-e734b811ce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28cb6212-1c96-5038-9cf1-1238bb9a8ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315352} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fdd9d97-878e-55f8-8858-b3a082f09eeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c8079ad-949a-5a26-9868-d6f6b2551b3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342296" :question/problem "$$\n(x+2)(x-1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=0$\n- $x=-2$\n- $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2)(x-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-2,$ since both denominators at $x=-2$ equal zero. So, $x=-2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=1,$ since both denominators at $x=1$ equal zero. So, $x=1$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae40602-954a-5af2-a16f-70c8ad4d9d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315369} {:answer/id #uuid "65e144ee-8cc6-5627-9fd5-800e85b51ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315684} {:answer/id #uuid "a2df0e86-4fc7-5151-9b72-843102b86f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316038} {:answer/id #uuid "a578df17-e347-574e-adf2-dc8917486d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316052} {:answer/id #uuid "fa833f8f-0902-57f8-a2ef-6721ba7656f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a578df17-e347-574e-adf2-dc8917486d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316052} :answer-field/id #uuid "b890767c-b1ed-5f63-bdea-2b15a7a09aaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fac58339-a2d8-5129-8e48-737c1bfcbe88" :question/math-academy-id "q-342299" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The functions $xP(x)$ and ${x}^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The point $x=0$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0,$ multiplying them by $x$ and ${x}^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=0$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c1e8f46-60a5-5fb3-9850-82cb6f2d4656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315991} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2a3487-9342-560e-9741-fc0184694c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316004} {:answer/id #uuid "a5560124-72e6-55d3-b566-e75819fa13c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316050} {:answer/id #uuid "b44b5861-5808-536f-ab0e-cc3df41096c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316138} {:answer/id #uuid "c454eb32-773b-5ff9-b580-875bef54d077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5560124-72e6-55d3-b566-e75819fa13c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316050} :answer-field/id #uuid "c35120b8-9f62-526d-9e83-a3ed24feb0c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "182bf2f2-0927-5526-ba0f-19a051c0f88c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342300" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The functions $xP(x)$ and ${x}^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The point $x=0$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by ${x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=0,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=0$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=0$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26764669-04e8-5301-9eac-da5e5a54d26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315336} {:answer/id #uuid "4672de51-4103-5eea-9cba-f59022d79f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315526} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea21f7d-14a2-5ce5-8e66-428875e18291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315743} {:answer/id #uuid "71025387-eb0d-580b-8156-6fd38dbace9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315756} {:answer/id #uuid "c65a4860-74